<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Semitic languages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> are an extinct branch of the Semitic language family, spoken in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. They include Akkadian, with its Babylonian and Assyrian dialects, and probably Eblaite, the language of the archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a>. Written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> from the mid-third millennium BCE, they are the earliest recorded Semitic languages, and Akkadian served as a language of international diplomacy in the second millennium BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-semitic-languages.jpg" alt="East Semitic languages" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Semitic_languages.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
